<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>France 24 - International breaking news, top stories and headlines</title><subtitle type="html">Breaking news and world news from France 24 on Business, Sports, Culture. Video news. News from the US, Europe, Asia Pacific, Africa, Middle East, America</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/rss</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>France wants curfews in some areas after weekend of riots</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e2ba9438c73bf473b9464f79c86573063da30767cf2993668a74c0ff469a3d88</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T15:37:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-france-wants-curfews-in-some-areas-after-weekend-of-riots" /><summary type="html">France's central government asked some local authorities to impose curfews and faced pressure over security failings on Monday after a weekend of riots and looting borne out of nationwide anti-government protests. Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu did not rule out declaring state of emergency but said it was more appropriate for now to apply partial and localised curfews, without providing details of which cities would be affected.</summary></entry><entry><title>France protests: Who are the people arrested during the unrest?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:78f37819bb26c3d7df9ee584e595e4355dce39ba930f665c8466ae40c469a2ac</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T15:36:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-france-protests-who-are-the-people-arrested-during-the-unrest" /><summary type="html">At least 2,400 people were arrested in France during protests over the weekend, some simply for being in the wrong place at the wrong time, others on suspicion of theft and looting. In an official address, French President Emmanuel Macron warned that “those who looted will have to pay for the damage”. But who exactly are these rioters?</summary></entry><entry><title>'Police weren't overwhelmed by people protesting and marching' about education and cost of living</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:098d5a1ed010a239d2b473917bffe4c0172d04e638a93bef22aeff093588e6fa</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T14:58:36Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-police-weren-t-overwhelmed-by-people-protesting-and-marching-about-education-and-cost-of-living" /><summary type="html">Oliver Farry is pleased to welcome Andrew Smith, Lecturer in Liberal Arts at Queen Mary University of London. France's escalating social unrest has exposed a troubling disconnect between legitimate popular grievances and the government's increasingly security-focused response. Smith argues that the violence accompanying otherwise largely peaceful demonstrations reveals serious failures of policing, while President Emmanuel Macron's response offers "very little of substance." Drawing parallels with France's historic protest movements, Smith identifies a "convergence of causes" rooted in economic insecurity, educational inequalities and political paralysis. He warns that a repressive response risks deepening the crisis, calling instead for a renewed commitment to "participatory social democracy."</summary></entry><entry><title>Four soldiers dead, dozens injured in fire at Canada military base</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:43bf3b61a18acf06a824abe566de7663cd749f848cb743ed3b22ece17ed7518d</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T14:25:09Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-four-soldiers-dead-dozens-injured-in-fire-at-canada-military-base" /><summary type="html">Four soldiers were killed and dozens injured in a fire at a military base in Alberta province, one of the worst peacetime tragedies to hit Canada's armed forces on home soil, Prime Minister Mark Carney said Sunday.</summary></entry><entry><title>French PM says 'ultra-left' seeking 'deep unrest' ahead of election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:23d334ae67b1f94c570ea1af289db94ce8d67089844c7f8ffbd545fd853e95f2</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T14:20:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-french-pm-says-ultra-left-seeking-deep-unrest-ahead-of-election" /><summary type="html">French Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu on Monday accused the "ultra-left movement" of seeking to "orchestrate deep unrest across the country" in the runup to next year's presidential election. FRANCE 24 International Affairs Editor Angela Diffley looks back at Lecornu's remarks in the wake of the violent protests that shook the country over the weekend.</summary></entry><entry><title>Brazil court orders Meta to prove it gives Lula, Bolsonaro campaign content equal treatment</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8661434997bef40d6875d7245e4b821aa7e3f76fecb983547746a0a00b7c2577</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T14:16:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261019-brazil-court-orders-meta-prove-gives-lula-bolsonaro-campaign-content-equal-treatment" /><summary type="html">Brazil’s top electoral court has given Meta 24 hours to prove it is treating campaign content from incumbent President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva and Senator Flavio Bolsonaro equally on its online platforms, threatening a daily fine for noncompliance. The ruling follows allegations from Lula's campaign that the social media giant is suppressing Lula’s campaign content ahead of Sunday’s closely contested presidential runoff.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>France protests draw global attention: Trump, Vance blame immigration for unrest</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:077907b144d8223f6ff63905aa275f231a53c6d33312faaa8c5e133e0f2f5a55</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T14:04:26Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-france-protests-draw-global-attention-trump-vance-blame-immigration-for-unrest" /><summary type="html">US President Donald Trump on Sunday blamed immigration for unrest during protests in France and urged the country to tighten its migration rules. Trump's post comes after similar comments made by Vice President JD Vance on Saturday. Vance, who is seen as a potential presidential candidate in the 2028 election, also drew a connection to upcoming midterm races for congressional seats on November 3, where Republicans are not expected to perform well. FRANCE 24's Fraser Jackson reports from the United States.</summary></entry><entry><title>Macron kicks off three-day Gulf tour, seeks securing fuel supplies amid crisis</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:dffca06d76721a4de6695903ce17eb52156e995279fa4d9322a13c1d1f05ae75</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T14:02:57Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-macron-kicks-off-three-day-gulf-tour-seeks-securing-fuel-supplies-amid-crisis" /><summary type="html">French President Emmanuel Macron on Monday began a Gulf tour aimed at securing oil and diesel supplies in an effort to bring down prices, after a weekend of violent protests sparked by soaring living costs. Macron, just hours after slamming France's "unbridled" violence in an address late on Sunday, arrived in OPEC member Kuwait where he was welcomed by the emir, Sheikh Mishal Al-Ahmad Al-Jaber Al-Sabah. FRANCE 24's International Affairs Editor Kethevane Gorjestani tells us more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Drone attacks kill scores in Sudan's West Kordofan state and capital Khartoum</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b51ea4b42bdde04ef6c86434c2e01516a277092a4ac2489a5360be44111d1273</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T14:00:04Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261019-drone-attack-near-southern-sudan-market-kills-scores" /><summary type="html">At least 80 people were killed in a drone strike near a market in Sudan’s southern West Kordofan state, a medical source and a witness said Monday, in one of the deadliest single attacks since the Sudan war erupted in 2023. A separate drone attack Monday in the capital Khartoum killed four, including three children.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>US midterms elections: Why data centers are becoming a key issue</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:224d872aab51a95a4f66f5bb740f9ce30c8477c6489c905ea5266ff4b4883c2f</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T13:59:29Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-us-midterms-elections-why-data-centers-are-becoming-a-key-issue" /><summary type="html">With the US midterm elections just weeks away, data centers are emerging as a major political issue for both Republicans and Democrats. There are about 3,000 facilities currently operating in the country. With that number potentially doubling in the coming years, concern – and, in some cases, anger – is growing among voters. FRANCE 24's Fraser Jackson report from West Virginia.</summary></entry><entry><title>France: Police and protesters clash violently over the weekend</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:386261f6db90d9a039266b72e5570aefb96b6d52765e0a922e734fce2eb53131</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T13:25:43Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-france-police-and-protesters-clash-violently-over-the-weekend" /><summary type="html">Hundreds of thousands took to the streets to protest higher living costs and the poor conditions in schools and other public services in France over the weekend. Police arrested nearly 2,400 people after violent groups clashed with officers and looted shops.The widespread unrest deepens political uncertainty in France as it prepares for elections in six months.</summary></entry><entry><title>French PM Lecornu says ready to declare state of emergency 'if needed'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b110259aa3f9691b894f70649f786773a2721d9b93bb44ad11b862840f3ec19e</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T12:51:21Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261019-french-pm-says-could-declare-state-of-emergency-over-unrest-if-needed" /><summary type="html">French Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu on Monday warned that he was prepared to declare a state of emergency as authorities struggled to contain ongoing violence after looting affected thousands of businesses over the weekend. The unrest linked to a student protest movement comes months before France’s presidential election, raising concerns over public security and political stability.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>France 'has to assist' Gulf states, diplomat says as Macron makes three-day visit</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d844c76df04cd056c1457efc5fe6a7569ca4f0d689966393f2516577b442dee6</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T12:43:41Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261019-france-has-to-assist-gulf-states-diplomat-says-as-macron-makes-three-day-visit" /><summary type="html">French President Emmanuel Macron is making a three-day visit to the Gulf states focused on fuel supplies and strategic ties. The French leader will first visit Kuwait before heading to the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and Qatar. The Elysée Palace said Macron would seek commitments for additional deliveries of diesel and petrol that could increase supplies available to France.</summary><category term="Perspective" /></entry><entry><title>Italy hit by violent cost-of-living protests, mostly minors arrested</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c598d13c3ee6d946bf85778175ee4c842f8fea569cd672fe5255c993bfd0e129</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T12:30:39Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-italy-hit-by-violent-cost-of-living-protests-mostly-minors-arrested" /><summary type="html">Youths and police clashed for a second consecutive day on Sunday in Milan, where at least 200 people were arrested during protests over the cost of living. Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni called a security meeting for Monday to address the violence, which evoked scenes of chaos and looting in Paris. FRANCE 24's Seema Gupta reports from Rome, Italy.</summary></entry><entry><title>REPLAY: French PM speaks after crisis meeting</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:986d2b1b87c82125f9044666fecfadddabb0bf954b1013503a55d5832866395b</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T12:21:24Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-replay-french-pm-speaks-after-crisis-meeting" /><summary type="html">French Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu on Monday said he had asked regional authorities to impose curfews in some cities. The measures, which he said would be "partial" and "localised", are intended to protect businesses after weekend demonstrations spilled over into street violence. Lecornu also accused the "ultra-left movement" of seeking to "orchestrate deep unrest across the country" in the runup to next year's presidential election. Watch our replay.</summary></entry><entry><title>Inflation squeezes households as Lebanon's economic crisis persists</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e324f58ea6a01115ca09694b4da98dc6887a381715a91511371d22973578e25d</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T12:18:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261019-inflation-squeezes-households-as-lebanon-s-economic-crisis-persists" /><summary type="html">Lebanon's economic crisis has dragged on since 2019. A re-igniting of Israel's war with Hezbollah derailed the weak recovery seen last year. The World Bank now forecasts that the Lebanese economy will shrink by 6.4 percent this year, with inflation expected to reach around 17.5 percent. A growing divide is visible everywhere: in households, in businesses and even in consumer habits. Our correspondents Serge Berberi and Renée Davis report.</summary><category term="Focus" /></entry><entry><title>Les Misérables: Macron's 'sense of detachment' amid rising living costs and youth 'fears for future'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b1da5cafdd9c802e469c841926c19adda98136b624fd8b7061d8f704c392402b</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T11:00:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-les-mis%C3%A9rables-macron-s-sense-of-detachment-amid-rising-living-costs-youth-fears-for-the-future" /><summary type="html">France’s escalating protest movement is exposing a widening disconnect between political leadership and public frustration, while raising questions about the country’s long tradition of social unrest. Chris Reynolds, Professor of French and European Studies at Nottingham Trent University, argues that President Emmanuel Macron’s latest address was characterised by a troubling “sense of detachment,” offering little to resolve the structural grievances driving the demonstrations. Drawing parallels with May 1968 and the Yellow Vest movement, Reynolds warns that violent unrest risks eclipsing the legitimate concerns of largely peaceful protesters, undermining public sympathy and reshaping political debate ahead of the presidential election.</summary></entry><entry><title>Marseille cleans up after looting and vandalism in protest</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:03cf35d2b2533ea2d670a80ae5b56cd475d26826de87d5fe0b09b92025062861</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T10:57:41Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-marseille-cleans-up-after-looting-and-vandalism-in-protest" /><summary type="html">Rubbish littered the streets of Marseille on Sunday, one day after demonstrations against rising living costs and declining public services spiralled out of control in some of the worst violence France has witnessed for decades. On Saturday, black-clad protesters broke into Marseille's city hall and defaced the building with graffiti, while public buildings were also targeted in other French cities.</summary></entry><entry><title>'Lame-duck president' Macron notes 'failures' after France unrest</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5685861174f32057c929ee7766021ee836e2aca17a11f95557f7e4831696789c</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T10:53:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-lame-duck-president-macron-notes-failures-after-france-unrest" /><summary type="html">French President Emmanuel Macron on Monday rejected Interior Minister Laurent Nunez's resignation. The move came in response to weekend protests, during which nearly 2,400 people were arrested and almost 1,000 police and gendarmes injured on Saturday. FRANCE 24's International affairs editor Philip Turle has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>French retailers reel after weekend of riots, looting</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fb1e34ffff60ebca2a76280c0d3f849bfa7ed25e419084934da30032492a91bf</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T10:29:21Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/business/20261019-french-retailers-reel-after-weekend-of-riots-looting" /><summary type="html">Representatives of the French business community gather for a crisis meeting with the government following widespread looting during the weekend's protests. Meanwhile, the country's tourism sector fears a drop in winter holiday bookings, and bond and currency traders sell off French debt and the euro.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Massive clean-up operation sweeps across France after protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:58b414974591758b729ceb6666a0f213521c2304fabc490673b9cf61e8181c8d</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T10:13:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-massive-clean-up-operation-sweeps-across-france-after-protests" /><summary type="html">A massive clean-up operation was under way across France on Monday, from Lille in the north to Marseille in the south and from Strasbourg in the east to Bordeaux in the southwest, following nationwide demonstrations last weekend. The protests over high prices and declining public services were followed by widespread looting and damage to shops, with thousands arrested. FRANCE 24's Luke Shrago reports from central Paris.</summary></entry><entry><title>'Shame', 'anger', 'chaos': Papers react to polarising riots in France</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d203d97b2eacc23c9c188cb33c3260a295d3f5bf60faad2092aa0bd733fa637c</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T10:11:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261019-shame-anger-chaos-papers-react-to-polarising-riots-in-france" /><summary type="html">PRESS REVIEW – Monday, October 19: French and international papers discuss the anti-government protests that turned violent at the weekend and their aftermath across France.</summary><category term="Press Review" /></entry><entry><title>French interior minister admits police 'lost control' of protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2542dc930d0ff0c81f9b0f25f36948e70f57bc29c5afb4a4e83a3acd1ad012c7</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T10:07:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-french-interior-minister-admits-police-lost-control-of-protests" /><summary type="html">France's Interior Minister Laurent Nunez admitted Monday that the police "lost control" in some cities during violent protests that erupted in cities across the country at the weekend. He said there had been a "lack of anticipation" in preparations and that he had tendered his resignation to President Emmanuel Macron "because I felt responsible for everything that happened." Macron did not accept his resignation.</summary></entry><entry><title>Protests continue sixth day in a row in Albania's capital</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:440e254e13a8f2fca45e15c625eb66592f09eee8cc4ad02755010c79ee17255a</id><updated>2026-10-19T15:44:05Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T10:03:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261019-protests-continue-sixth-day-in-a-row-in-albania-s-capital" /><summary type="html">Albania's capital saw protests Sunday for a sixth day in a row as Prime Minister Edi Rama ruled out resigning, the main demand of the protesters. Violent demonstrations have rocked Tirana since Tuesday, with thousands of protesters calling for Rama to step down as they rally against poor university conditions, high tuition fees and corruption.</summary></entry></feed>
